export const A6={title:'Heart, lungs and vital signs (Skill VII)',hook:'Skill VII is the largest skill in the event—81 points—and asks you to measure temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure smoothly in ten minutes, with pulse and respirations within ±2 and blood pressure within ±2 mm Hg of the judge. Vital signs also reveal how the heart and lungs are working.',objectives:['Describe the cardiovascular and respiratory systems and common disorders.','State normal adult vital sign ranges and factors that affect them.','Measure TPR and aneroid blood pressure following Skill VII.','Recognize abnormal readings and report them.'],sections:[
['The cardiovascular and respiratory systems',[
'The **heart** pumps blood through **arteries** (away from the heart), **capillaries** (where exchange occurs) and **veins** (back to the heart). The right side pumps blood to the lungs; the left side pumps oxygen-rich blood to the body. The **respiratory system** brings air through the nose, pharynx, larynx, trachea and bronchi to the **alveoli**, where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves. Common disorders include **hypertension**, **coronary artery disease** and **angina**, **myocardial infarction** (heart attack—chest pain or pressure, shortness of breath, sweating, nausea; report immediately), **heart failure** (swelling, weight gain, shortness of breath—weigh daily at the same time), **peripheral vascular disease**, **COPD** (emphysema and chronic bronchitis), **asthma** and **pneumonia**. Residents with breathing problems are positioned upright (Fowler’s or orthopneic), use pursed-lip breathing and need oxygen safety—no smoking or flames, and nursing assistants never adjust the oxygen flow rate.',
label('heart','The heart',[['ra','Right atrium','Receives blood from the body.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs.'],['la','Left atrium','Receives blood from the lungs.'],['lv','Left ventricle','Pumps blood to the body.'],['aorta','Aorta','Largest artery.'],['mitral','Mitral valve','Between left atrium and ventricle.']]),
label('resp','The respiratory system',[['trachea','Trachea','Windpipe.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Airways to each lung.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Air sacs for gas exchange.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Main breathing muscle.']])]],
['Normal ranges and influences',[
'Adult teaching ranges: **oral temperature** about **97.6–99.6°F** (rectal and tympanic slightly higher, axillary lower); **pulse 60–100** beats per minute; **respirations 12–20** per minute; **blood pressure** below **120/80 mm Hg** is normal, with hypertension beginning at 130/80 under current guidelines. Factors that raise readings include exercise, pain, fever, anxiety, eating, smoking and caffeine; sleep and some medications lower them. Ask the resident about **eating, drinking and smoking** before an oral temperature, because these change mouth temperature—wait as facility policy directs (often 15–20 minutes). **Tachycardia** is a pulse above 100, **bradycardia** below 60; **tachypnea** is rapid breathing and **apnea** is the absence of breathing. Describe the pulse by **rate, rhythm (regular or irregular) and volume (strong or weak)**, and respirations by **rate, depth and rhythm**.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which readings fall within the usual adult teaching ranges and which you would report to the nurse.',person:'resident',reportTo:'nurse'}]],
['Temperature, pulse and respirations (Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII (10 minutes), the competitor assembles equipment, performs hand hygiene, greets, identifies and explains, **asks about eating, drinking and smoking** and positions the resident comfortably. **Temperature (electronic):** place the thermometer in a **disposable sheath** or put a probe cover on the probe; ask the resident to open the mouth and lift the tongue; slowly insert the probe until the tip touches the **base of the mouth under the tongue to one side** (the sublingual pocket); ask the resident to close the mouth; hold it until the unit signals; remove, **read and verbalize** the temperature; discard the cover in a biohazard container without touching it; return the thermometer; and **record** on the graphic form. **Radial pulse:** support the hand and arm, place the **middle and index fingers** on the inside of the wrist on the **thumb side**, use light pressure and **count for a full minute**. **Respirations:** keep the fingers in pulse position so the resident is unaware and **count for a full minute**. Verbalize counts **within ±2** of the judge’s count, **describe pulse quality (volume and rhythm) and respirations (depth and rhythm)** and record accurately.',
order('Temperature, pulse and respirations in Skill VII',['Ask about eating, drinking and smoking; position comfortably','Cover the probe and place it under the tongue to one side','Hold until it signals; read, verbalize and discard the cover','Support the arm; place two fingers on the thumb side of the wrist','Count the radial pulse for one full minute','Keep fingers in place and count respirations for one full minute','Report counts and describe quality to the judge','Record on the graphic form'])]],
['Aneroid blood pressure (Skill VII)',[
'For **blood pressure**, the competitor **cleans the stethoscope earpieces and diaphragm**, exposes the upper arm and positions it **level with the heart with the palm up**, opens the valve and squeezes the cuff to **deflate it completely**, locates the **brachial artery** with the fingertips and wraps the cuff **snugly and smoothly about 1 inch above the elbow** with the center over the brachial artery. They **close the valve clockwise**, place the earpieces forward in the ears, palpate the brachial artery and place the stethoscope over it, **inflate the cuff to 180 mm Hg**, then **deflate slowly counterclockwise at an even 2–4 mm Hg per second**, noting the **first sound (systolic)** and **last sound (diastolic)**. They deflate completely, remove the cuff, make the resident comfortable, **record** the reading, clean the earpieces and replace equipment, with **systolic and diastolic within ±2 mm Hg** of the judge. The skill ends with hand hygiene, performing all vital signs **smoothly and in logical order** (overlapping steps for efficiency), observing **checkpoints**—call signal within reach, bed lowered, resident in good alignment, changes reported—and communication and standard precautions (81 points; 70% is 56.7).',
order('Aneroid blood pressure in Skill VII',['Clean the earpieces and diaphragm','Position the bare arm at heart level, palm up','Deflate the cuff completely and locate the brachial artery','Wrap the cuff snugly about 1 inch above the elbow, centered on the artery','Close the valve; place the stethoscope over the brachial artery','Inflate to 180 mm Hg','Deflate 2–4 mm Hg per second; note first and last sounds','Deflate fully, remove the cuff and record'])]],
['Recognizing problems',[
kase('An abnormal reading',{a:{text:'A resident’s blood pressure is 182/104, and she says she has a headache and blurred vision. What should you do?',choices:[['Report to the nurse immediately and stay with the resident','b','Correct. A very high reading with symptoms needs prompt attention.',true],['Record it and report at the end of the shift','b','Delay could be dangerous.',false]]},b:{text:'You realize you used an adult cuff that was too small for her arm. How might that affect the reading?',choices:[['A cuff that is too small can give a falsely high reading; recheck with the correct size','c','Correct. Cuff size affects accuracy.',true],['Cuff size has no effect','c','Too small reads high; too large reads low.',false]]},c:{text:'You recheck with the correct cuff, report both readings and symptoms to the nurse and document.',end:true}})]],
["Other measurements: temperature sites, oxygen, height, weight and pain",["Temperature can also be measured at other sites. **Tympanic** thermometers are quick (pull the adult ear up and back); **temporal artery** thermometers glide across the forehead; **axillary** readings are lower and less accurate; and **rectal** readings are the most accurate but are not used for residents with diarrhea, rectal disease or surgery, heart conditions as directed, or who are combative. Record the site with each reading. **Pulse oximetry** estimates oxygen saturation (normally about **95–100%** in healthy adults); nail polish, cold fingers and movement can affect readings. The **apical pulse**, counted for a full minute with a stethoscope over the apex of the heart, is used for irregular rhythms.","**Height and weight** are measured on admission and as ordered—**weigh at the same time of day, on the same scale, in similar clothing**, often before breakfast after voiding—and report sudden gains or losses. **Pain** is often called the fifth vital sign: ask residents to rate pain from 0 to 10, use faces scales when helpful, and watch for **nonverbal signs**—grimacing, guarding, moaning, restlessness or changes in behavior, especially in residents with dementia—and report pain promptly.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Accurate technique or likely error?","buckets":["Accurate technique","Likely to cause an error"],"items":[["Weighing at the same time on the same scale",0],["Using a rectal thermometer on a resident with diarrhea",1],["Recording the temperature site",0],["Placing a pulse oximeter on a cold finger with dark nail polish",1],["Counting an irregular apical pulse for one minute",0],["Weighing a resident in heavy boots one day and a gown the next",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Adult teaching ranges: oral temperature about 97.6–99.6°F, pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, BP below 120/80; ask about eating, drinking and smoking first.','Skill VII TPR: probe under the tongue to one side, radial pulse and respirations each for a full minute, within ±2, describe quality.','Skill VII BP: arm at heart level palm up, cuff 1 inch above the elbow, inflate to 180, deflate 2–4 mm Hg per second, first and last sounds within ±2 mm Hg.'],terms:[['Systolic pressure','Pressure when the heart contracts (first sound).'],['Diastolic pressure','Pressure when the heart relaxes (last sound).'],['Bradycardia','Heart rate below 60 beats per minute.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill VII, the radial pulse is counted for:','One full minute','15 seconds','30 seconds','10 seconds','Respirations are also counted for a full minute.'),q('The radial pulse is found on the:','Thumb side of the inner wrist','Little-finger side of the wrist','Neck','Top of the foot','Use the middle and index fingers.'),q('Why ask about eating, drinking and smoking before an oral temperature?','They can change the mouth temperature','To plan meals','To check allergies','It is not necessary','Wait as policy directs.'),q('In Skill VII, the cuff is inflated to:','180 mm Hg','100 mm Hg','250 mm Hg','300 mm Hg','Then deflated slowly.'),q('The cuff is deflated at a rate of:','2–4 mm Hg per second','10–20 mm Hg per second','1 mm Hg per minute','All at once','An even, slow rate improves accuracy.'),q('The first sound heard is the:','Systolic pressure','Diastolic pressure','Pulse pressure','Mean pressure','The last sound is diastolic.'),q('A pulse of 112 is called:','Tachycardia','Bradycardia','Apnea','Normal','Above 100 in adults.'),q('The cuff is placed about how far above the elbow?','1 inch','6 inches','At the wrist','Over the elbow','Center it over the brachial artery.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would measure and record TPR and an aneroid blood pressure following HOSA Skill VII, and explain which findings you would report.',rubric:[['Preparation and temperature',['eating','drinking','smoking','probe cover','under the tongue','one side','signal']],['Pulse and respirations',['radial','thumb side','full minute','unaware','quality','rhythm','depth']],['Blood pressure technique',['heart level','palm','1 inch','brachial','180','2–4','first','last']],['Recording and reporting',['graphic','record','±2','report','nurse','checkpoint','call signal']]],model:'After hand hygiene, greeting, identifying and explaining, I would ask about eating, drinking and smoking and position the resident comfortably. I would cover the electronic thermometer probe, have the resident lift the tongue, place the probe at the base of the mouth under the tongue to one side, have them close their mouth, hold until it signals, then read and state the temperature, discard the cover without touching it and record it. With the arm supported, I would place my middle and index fingers on the thumb side of the inner wrist and count the radial pulse for a full minute, then keep my fingers in place and count respirations for a full minute so the resident is unaware, reporting counts within two of the judge’s and describing pulse volume and rhythm and breathing depth and rhythm. For blood pressure, I would clean the stethoscope, position the bare arm at heart level palm up, deflate and wrap the cuff about 1 inch above the elbow centered over the brachial artery, close the valve, place the stethoscope over the artery, inflate to 180 mm Hg and deflate at 2–4 mm Hg per second, noting the first (systolic) and last (diastolic) sounds, then remove the cuff and record. I would complete checkpoints—call signal within reach, bed lowered, good alignment—and report to the nurse any reading outside the normal ranges or a change from baseline, especially with symptoms such as chest pain, headache or shortness of breath.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VITALS,AHA_BP,NHLBI_COPD]};

export const A7={title:'Digestion, nutrition, elimination and ostomy care (Skill VIII)',hook:'Which diet is often prescribed for chronic constipation, irritable bowel syndrome or a higher risk of colon cancer? A high-fiber diet. Nutrition, fluids and elimination are daily nursing assistant work—and Skill VIII asks you to change an ostomy pouch with skill and dignity.',objectives:['Describe the digestive, urinary and endocrine systems and common disorders.','Explain therapeutic diets, fluid balance and feeding assistance.','Measure intake and output and care for residents with catheters.','Perform ostomy care following HOSA Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['Digestive, urinary and endocrine systems',[
'The **digestive system** breaks down food from the mouth through the esophagus, stomach, small intestine (most absorption) and large intestine (water absorption) to the rectum, helped by the liver, gallbladder and pancreas. Disorders include **GERD**, **constipation**, **diarrhea**, **diverticulitis**, **irritable bowel syndrome** and **colorectal cancer**. The **urinary system**—kidneys, ureters, bladder and urethra—filters blood and removes wastes; disorders include **urinary tract infections**, **incontinence**, **retention**, **kidney stones** and **chronic kidney disease**. The **endocrine system** uses hormones to regulate the body; in **diabetes mellitus**, the body does not make or use insulin properly. Watch for **hypoglycemia** (shaky, sweaty, confused, cool clammy skin) and **hyperglycemia** (thirst, frequent urination, fatigue), serve meals on time and give careful **foot care**—nursing assistants do not cut diabetic residents’ toenails.',
label('gi','The digestive system',[['mouth','Mouth','Chewing and saliva.'],['esophagus','Esophagus','Carries food to the stomach.'],['stomach','Stomach','Mixes food with acid.'],['smallint','Small intestine','Absorbs nutrients.'],['largeint','Large intestine','Absorbs water; site of a colostomy.'],['rectum','Rectum','Stores stool.']])]],
['Nutrition and therapeutic diets',[
'**MyPlate** guides balanced meals with fruits, vegetables, grains, protein foods and dairy. Residents may need **therapeutic diets** ordered by the provider: a **high-fiber diet** for chronic constipation, irritable bowel syndrome or elevated colon cancer risk; **low-sodium** for hypertension and heart failure; **carbohydrate-controlled** for diabetes; **low-fat/low-cholesterol** for heart disease; **fluid-restricted** for kidney or heart failure; **mechanical soft** or **pureed** for chewing problems; and **thickened liquids** for **dysphagia** (swallowing problems). When assisting with meals, check the **diet card** against the resident’s identification, position the resident **upright** (90° when possible), offer hand hygiene, sit at eye level, offer **small bites** and sips, allow time to chew and swallow, encourage independence with adaptive utensils and watch for **coughing or choking**. Record the percentage of the meal eaten and report poor appetite or difficulty swallowing.',
match('Match the diet to the resident',[['High-fiber diet','Resident with chronic constipation'],['Low-sodium diet','Resident with heart failure'],['Carbohydrate-controlled diet','Resident with diabetes'],['Thickened liquids','Resident with dysphagia'],['Fluid restriction','Resident with kidney failure'],['Pureed diet','Resident who cannot chew']]),
chk('Which diet is often prescribed for chronic constipation, irritable bowel syndrome or elevated colon cancer risk?',['High-fiber','Low-cholesterol','High-fat','Low-fiber'],'A Roe sample question.')]],
['Fluids, intake and output and catheters',[
'Most adults need about **64 ounces (about 2,000 mL)** of fluid daily unless restricted. **Dehydration** signs include dark, concentrated urine, dry mouth, confusion and constipation; **edema** and sudden weight gain suggest fluid overload. **Intake and output (I&O)** is measured in **milliliters**; **1 ounce = 30 mL**. Intake includes liquids and foods liquid at room temperature (gelatin, ice cream, soup); output includes urine, emesis, liquid stool and drainage. Measure output in a **graduate** on a flat surface at eye level. For residents with an **indwelling catheter**: keep the drainage bag **below the bladder** and **off the floor**, keep tubing free of kinks, never disconnect the closed system, secure the tubing to the thigh, provide **catheter care** (cleaning around the urethral opening and down the tubing away from the body) and report blood, cloudy or foul-smelling urine, leaking, pain or low output. Encourage toileting on a schedule to prevent incontinence and provide prompt perineal care.',
{t:'w',kind:'iolab',title:'Total intake and output',prompt:'Convert ounces to millilitres and round output readings between lines up to the nearest 25 mL.'}]],
['Ostomy care (Skill VIII)',[
'An **ostomy** is a surgically created opening (**stoma**) on the abdomen for elimination: a **colostomy** from the large intestine (stool is often formed) or an **ileostomy** from the small intestine (liquid stool that irritates skin). A healthy stoma is **moist and pink to red**; report a stoma that is pale, dark, swollen or bleeding, and skin irritation. In Skill VIII (8 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, **knocks before entering**, greets, identifies, explains and **provides privacy**, raises the bed to **hip level** and locks the wheels, lowers the side rails as indicated, performs hand hygiene and puts on **non-latex gloves**. They place the resident on the back, put a **protective pad under the buttocks**, cover the resident with a **bath blanket**, fanfold the top linens and move clothing to expose the stoma, and place a **second pad alongside** the body, covering the resident from the waist down. They **pull gently on one edge of the pouch to release air**, remove the pouch carefully into a **plastic bag**, wipe around the stoma with a disposable wipe, wash with a washcloth and warm water **in one direction away from the stoma**, rinse and pat dry, and **temporarily cover the stoma** with a wipe. They apply deodorant if indicated, apply the **clean pouch**, holding it in place to secure the seal, and **clamp the bottom**. They remove and discard the protector, linens and bag, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene, make the resident comfortable, **lower the bed**, remove privacy measures, leave the **call light within reach**, perform hand hygiene, **document** the **color, odor, consistency and amount of stool** and **report changes** in the stoma or resident’s condition (53 points; 70% is 37.1).',
order('Ostomy care (HOSA Skill VIII)',['Knock, greet, identify, explain and provide privacy','Raise the bed to hip level, lock wheels; hand hygiene and gloves','Place a pad under the buttocks and drape with a bath blanket','Expose the stoma and place a second pad alongside','Release air and remove the pouch into a plastic bag','Wipe, then wash away from the stoma; rinse and pat dry','Apply the clean pouch, secure the seal and clamp the bottom','Clean up, lower the bed, call light within reach, document and report'])]],
['Applying the skills',[
kase('A stoma change',{a:{text:'While changing an ostomy pouch, you notice the stoma looks dark purple instead of its usual red color. What should you do?',choices:[['Finish safely, then report the color change to the nurse immediately and document it','b','Correct. A dark stoma can signal poor blood supply.',true],['Ignore it because stomas vary','b','A change from baseline must be reported.',false]]},b:{text:'The resident is embarrassed and quiet during the procedure. How do you respond?',choices:[['Maintain privacy, explain each step and speak respectfully','c','Correct. Dignity is part of ostomy care.',true],['Comment on the odor','c','Avoid remarks that cause embarrassment.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse assesses the stoma promptly, and you document the stool characteristics and the resident’s reaction.',end:true}})]],
["Collecting specimens and catheter care",["Nursing assistants often collect **specimens**. A **routine urine** specimen is collected in a clean container; a **clean-catch (midstream)** specimen requires cleansing the urethral area, starting to void, then collecting the middle of the stream in a sterile container; a **24-hour** specimen begins after the resident empties the bladder and that urine is discarded, then **all** urine for 24 hours is collected (usually on ice or refrigerated) and the last void is included. **Stool** specimens are collected with a tongue blade into a specimen container without urine or toilet paper; **sputum** specimens are best collected early in the morning after the resident rinses the mouth (no mouthwash) and coughs deeply from the lungs. Label every specimen with the resident’s name, room, date and time, place it in a biohazard bag and take it promptly to the designated area.","**Catheter care** prevents infection: with gloves, clean the perineal area, then clean the catheter from the urethral opening **outward about 4 inches** with a clean area of the washcloth for each stroke, rinse and dry; check that the tubing is not kinked, the bag is below the bladder and off the floor and the catheter is secured to the thigh. Report blood, cloudy or foul-smelling urine, leaking, pain or low output.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Collect a clean-catch urine specimen","steps":["Explain and provide supplies and privacy","Have the resident cleanse the urethral area","Begin voiding into the toilet or bedpan","Collect the middle of the stream in the sterile container","Finish voiding and cap the container without touching the inside","Label and bag the specimen; take it promptly to the lab area"]}]]
],takeaways:['High-fiber diets are prescribed for chronic constipation, IBS or colon cancer risk; check diet cards, position upright and record the percentage eaten.','1 oz = 30 mL; keep catheter bags below the bladder and off the floor; report low output, blood or cloudy urine.','Skill VIII: release air, remove into a bag, wash away from the stoma, pat dry, apply and clamp the new pouch; document color, odor, consistency and amount; a healthy stoma is moist and pink to red.'],terms:[['Stoma','Opening on the abdomen created for an ostomy.'],['Dysphagia','Difficulty swallowing.'],['Therapeutic diet','A diet ordered to treat or manage a condition.']],mcqs:[q('Which diet is often prescribed for chronic constipation or irritable bowel syndrome?','High-fiber','Low-fiber','High-fat','Low-cholesterol','A Roe sample question.'),q('A healthy stoma is:','Moist and pink to red','Dry and black','Pale white','Purple and swollen','Report color changes.'),q('In Skill VIII, the area around the stoma is washed:','In one direction away from the stoma','Toward the stoma','In circles over the stoma','It is not washed','Prevents contamination.'),q('Before removing the old pouch, the competitor:','Gently pulls one edge to release air','Cuts the pouch','Fills it with water','Removes the clamp only','Then removes it carefully.'),q('What must be documented after ostomy care?','Color, odor, consistency and amount of stool','Only the time','The resident’s visitors','Nothing','These observations are scored.'),q('One ounce equals:','30 mL','15 mL','5 mL','100 mL','Use for intake calculations.'),q('A urinary drainage bag should be kept:','Below the bladder and off the floor','On the floor','Above the bladder','On the bed','Prevents backflow.'),q('Which liquid diet modification is used for dysphagia?','Thickened liquids','Carbonated drinks','Extra-thin liquids','Ice water only','Reduces aspiration risk.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would perform ostomy care following HOSA Skill VIII and explain the nutrition and fluid care you would provide for a resident with a new colostomy.',rubric:[['Preparation and privacy',['knock','privacy','identify','hip level','gloves','bath blanket','pad']],['Pouch change technique',['release air','plastic bag','away from the stoma','pat dry','seal','clamp']],['Observation and documentation',['color','odor','consistency','amount','stoma','report']],['Nutrition and fluids',['fluid','fiber','diet','intake','output','30 ml']]],model:'After assembling supplies and performing hand hygiene, I would knock, greet and identify the resident, explain the procedure and provide privacy, raise the bed to hip level, lock the wheels, lower the side rail, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves. I would place the resident on the back with a protective pad under the buttocks, cover them with a bath blanket, fanfold the linens and expose the stoma, placing a second pad alongside. I would gently pull one edge of the pouch to release air, remove it into a plastic bag, wipe around the stoma, wash with warm water in one direction away from the stoma, rinse and pat dry, cover the stoma briefly with a wipe, apply deodorant if indicated and apply the clean pouch, holding it to secure the seal and clamping the bottom. After cleanup, I would remove gloves, perform hand hygiene, lower the bed, leave the call light within reach and document the color, odor, consistency and amount of stool and the condition of the stoma, reporting a stoma that is dark, pale, swollen or bleeding. For nutrition, I would follow the ordered diet, encourage fluids unless restricted—often about 64 ounces daily—offer fiber as allowed to keep stool soft, record intake and output in mL using 1 ounce equals 30 mL and report poor intake, dehydration or skin irritation.'},sources:[RULES,NIDDK_DIG,UOAA,MYPLATE]};

export const A8={title:'The resident’s unit: admission and bedmaking (Skills III, IV and V)',hook:'The first hour in a new facility shapes how safe and welcome a resident feels. A careful admission, a clean, smooth bed and a call light within reach are simple acts of care—and three Round Two skills worth 118 points together.',objectives:['Admit a resident and orient them to the room and facility following Skill V.','Make an unoccupied (closed) bed following Skill III.','Make an occupied bed following Skill IV.','Maintain a safe, comfortable resident unit.'],sections:[
['Admitting a resident (Skill V)',[
'Admission can be stressful; residents may feel anxious about losing independence. In Skill V (8 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, greets the resident and introduces themselves, **identifies the resident and places an identification bracelet** on the wrist, explains the procedure and **provides privacy**. They obtain the **admission paperwork** and hand it to the supervisor (judge), **verify that belongings are in the room** and **ask whether any valuables should be placed in the safe**. They **orient the resident to the room**—the purpose of items on the bedside table, how to use the **overbed table**, the **call light**, bed controls, TV and light controls, how to make phone calls (placing the phone within reach) and the **bathroom and its emergency call light**—and **to the facility**: visiting hours and policies, how to find the nurses’ station, chapel and dining room, how to identify staff by clothing or ID badges, and meal times and how to request snacks. They make sure the resident is comfortable with the **call light within reach**, conduct a **safety check**, perform hand hygiene, communicate appropriately, **report concerns** to the nurse and **document** the admission (36 points; 70% is 25.2). Many facilities also measure baseline vital signs, height and weight and complete a belongings inventory.',
order('Admitting a resident (HOSA Skill V)',['Hand hygiene; greet and introduce yourself','Identify the resident and apply an ID bracelet','Explain and provide privacy','Give admission paperwork to the supervisor','Check belongings and offer the safe for valuables','Orient to the room: tables, call light, bed, TV, phone, bathroom call light','Orient to the facility: visiting, locations, staff ID, meals','Call light within reach, safety check, report and document'])]],
['Bedmaking principles',[
'A clean, smooth, dry bed prevents skin breakdown and infection. Principles: perform hand hygiene and wear gloves if linens are soiled; **lock the bed wheels** and raise the bed to a comfortable working height; collect linens in the **order of use** and never bring extra linen into a room on isolation (unused linen taken into a room is considered contaminated); **hold linens away from your uniform**; **roll soiled linen inward** (dirty side in) and place it directly in the hamper—never on the floor or furniture, and never shake linens; **work on one side** of the bed until it is complete to save time and steps; make linens **tight and wrinkle-free**; use **mitered corners**; and finish with the bed in the **lowest position**, wheels locked and the call light within reach. A **closed bed** has the top linens pulled up to the head; an **open bed** has them fanfolded down, ready for the resident.',
sort('Correct bedmaking practice?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Rolling soiled linens with the dirty side in',0],['Shaking linens to remove crumbs',1],['Placing soiled linens directly in the hamper',0],['Setting soiled linens on the floor',1],['Finishing one side of the bed before the other',0],['Holding linens against your uniform',1]])]],
['Making an unoccupied bed (Skill III)',[
'In Skill III (8 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene and puts on gloves if indicated, arranges items in **order of use**, **locks the wheels** and raises the bed. They remove soiled linens by **rolling edges inward** without letting them touch the scrubs, deposit them in the hamper, **work on one side until complete**, and remove pillows, placing pillowcases in the hamper. They apply the **fitted bottom sheet**, pulling corners tightly over the mattress; place the **drawsheet** (or an incontinence pad) at the center and tuck it tightly; unfold the **top sheet wrong side up** with the hem even with the top edge of the mattress, using the center fold to center it; place the **spread or blanket**, centered; **tuck the top sheet and blanket under the mattress at the foot**; make a **mitered corner** and tuck in the sides; and **fold the top sheet back over the blanket to make a 6-inch cuff**. They insert pillows into pillowcases and place them at the head with the **open end away from the door**, check that the wheels are locked and **lower the bed to the lowest position**, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene, **place the call light on the bed**, replace moved items, conduct a **safety check**, perform hand hygiene and follow standard precautions (33 points; 70% is 23.1).',
order('Unoccupied bed (HOSA Skill III)',['Gather linens in order of use; hand hygiene; lock and raise the bed','Roll soiled linens inward into the hamper','Apply the fitted bottom sheet tightly','Place and tuck the drawsheet at the center','Place the top sheet wrong side up, hem even with the mattress top','Center the spread; tuck at the foot and miter the corners','Fold a 6-inch cuff; pillows with open ends away from the door','Lower the bed, place the call light and do a safety check'])]],
['Making an occupied bed (Skill IV)',[
'When a resident cannot get out of bed, the bed is made with them in it while protecting privacy, safety and comfort. In Skill IV (10 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene and gloves if indicated, greets, identifies and explains, **locks the wheels and raises the bed to hip level**, lowers the side rail on the working side, arranges linen in order of use, removes the call light and flattens the bed unless the care plan says otherwise. They loosen the top linen, place a **bath blanket** over the resident (who holds the edge or has it tucked under the shoulders) and remove the top linens **without exposing the resident**. They help the resident **roll away** to the far side and grasp the side rail, move the pillow with them, **loosen the soiled bottom linens from the head and tuck them against the resident’s back**, place the **fitted sheet** with its center crease in the center, pull its corners tightly and **roll the extra material toward the resident**, and place the **drawsheet**, tucked and **fanfolded to the center**. They **turn the resident toward themselves**, explaining that the resident will roll over the linen, **raise the side rail**, move to the other side and lower that rail, remove soiled linen by **rolling it from head to foot**, avoiding skin and clothing, place it in the hamper, **pull the clean linen through quickly**, pull the fitted sheet tightly over the corners and tuck the drawsheet without wrinkles. The resident returns to the center on the back and the rail is raised; pillowcases are changed; the **top sheet** is centered over the bath blanket, which is pulled out while the resident holds the sheet; the spread is added, tucked and **mitered**, with **toe pleats** if needed, and a **6-inch cuff** is folded. The competitor observes **checkpoints**—call signal within reach, bed lowered, resident in good alignment and changes reported—then removes gloves, performs hand hygiene and communicates throughout (49 points; 70% is 34.3).',
order('Occupied bed (HOSA Skill IV)',['Lock wheels, raise to hip level, lower the working-side rail','Cover the resident with a bath blanket; remove top linens','Roll the resident away; tuck soiled linen against the back','Place the fitted sheet and fanfolded drawsheet; roll extra toward the resident','Roll the resident toward you over the linen; raise the rail','On the other side, remove soiled linen head to foot','Pull clean linen through and tighten it','Return the resident to center; change pillowcases; top sheet, spread, miters, toe pleats and cuff; checkpoints'])]],
['A safe resident unit',[
'The resident’s unit usually includes the **bed**, **overbed table** (for meals and activities—not for bedpans or soiled items), **bedside stand**, **call light**, **chair**, lighting, privacy curtain, storage and a bathroom with an emergency call light. Keep the unit clean, uncluttered and at a comfortable temperature; place personal items, water and the **call light within reach**; respond to call lights promptly; and respect the resident’s belongings and arrangement of the room—it is their home. Before leaving any room, complete **checkpoints**: call light within reach, bed in the lowest position with wheels locked, side rails as ordered, resident in good alignment and comfortable, and any changes reported.',
kase('Before you leave',{a:{text:'You finish making an occupied bed and are about to leave. The bed is still at hip level and the call light is clipped to the curtain. What should you do?',choices:[['Lower the bed, place the call light within reach and check alignment before leaving','b','Correct. These are required checkpoints.',true],['Leave—the nurse will fix it later','b','Leaving the bed high and the call light out of reach risks falls.',false]]},b:{text:'The resident asks you to move a family photo onto the overbed table. Is that acceptable?',choices:[['Yes—respect the resident’s preference while keeping the table clean for meals','c','Correct. The room is the resident’s home.',true],['No—personal items are not allowed','c','Residents have the right to keep personal possessions.',false]]},c:{text:'You move the photo, complete your checkpoints and document the bed change.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Skill V: ID bracelet, privacy, paperwork to the supervisor, belongings and valuables, room and facility orientation, call light within reach, safety check, report and document.','Skill III: roll linens inward, one side at a time, top sheet wrong side up, mitered corners, 6-inch cuff, pillow open end away from the door, lowest position.','Skill IV: bath blanket for privacy, roll away then toward, fanfold the drawsheet, remove soiled linen head to foot, toe pleats and checkpoints.'],terms:[['Mitered corner','A neat, folded corner that keeps linens tucked tightly.'],['Closed bed','Bed with top linens pulled up, not ready for immediate use.'],['Checkpoints','Safety checks completed before leaving a resident.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill V, what is placed on the resident’s wrist after identification?','An identification bracelet','A gait belt','A watch','A blood pressure cuff','It confirms identity.'),q('In Skill III, the top sheet is placed:','Wrong side up with the hem even with the top of the mattress','Right side up at the foot','Under the fitted sheet','Folded in quarters','It is folded into a cuff later.'),q('How big is the cuff folded over the blanket?','6 inches','1 inch','12 inches','2 feet','Rating sheets specify 6 inches.'),q('Pillows are placed with the open end:','Away from the door','Toward the door','Toward the window','Facing up','A neat, finished appearance.'),q('Soiled linens should be:','Rolled inward and placed in the hamper','Shaken out','Placed on the floor','Carried against the uniform','Prevents spreading microorganisms.'),q('In Skill IV, the resident is first covered with a:','Bath blanket','Fitted sheet','Gown only','Towel','Protects privacy while top linens are removed.'),q('In Skill IV, soiled bottom linen is removed by rolling it:','From head to foot','Foot to head','Side to side over the resident','It is not removed','Avoid contact with skin and clothing.'),q('Before leaving a resident’s room, the bed should be:','In the lowest position with wheels locked','At the highest position','Unlocked','Raised to hip level','A key safety checkpoint.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would admit a new resident following HOSA Skill V, then make their bed while they rest in it following Skill IV, protecting privacy and safety throughout.',rubric:[['Admission steps',['identification bracelet','privacy','paperwork','belongings','valuables','safe']],['Orientation',['call light','bed controls','overbed','bathroom','visiting','meals','staff']],['Occupied bed technique',['bath blanket','roll','fanfold','head to foot','pull through','miter','toe pleat']],['Safety and documentation',['side rail','lowest','call light within reach','alignment','report','document']]],model:'For admission, I would perform hand hygiene, greet the resident and introduce myself, identify them and apply an ID bracelet, explain the process and provide privacy. I would give the admission paperwork to the supervisor, check that belongings are in the room and offer to place valuables in the safe. I would orient them to the room—the bedside table and overbed table, call light, bed controls, TV and light controls, telephone within reach and the bathroom with its emergency call light—and to the facility, including visiting hours, the nurses’ station, chapel and dining room, how to recognize staff and meal and snack times. Later, to make the occupied bed, I would lock the wheels, raise the bed to hip level, lower the working-side rail, cover the resident with a bath blanket and remove the top linens without exposing them. I would help them roll away, tuck the soiled linens against their back, apply the fitted sheet and a fanfolded drawsheet, roll them toward me over the linens and raise the rail, then on the other side remove soiled linen from head to foot and pull the clean linen through tightly. With the resident back in the center, I would change the pillowcases, place the top sheet and spread, remove the bath blanket, miter the corners, add toe pleats and fold a 6-inch cuff. Before leaving, I would lower the bed, keep the call light within reach, check alignment, report concerns and document both skills.'},sources:[RULES,CMS_RIGHTS]};

export const A9={title:'Providing and reporting personal care',hook:'Providing and reporting care is 30% of the test—the largest area. Bathing, oral care, dressing, toileting and feeding are not just tasks: each is a chance to protect skin, prevent infection, preserve dignity and notice changes that the nurse needs to know.',objectives:['Provide bathing, perineal care and skin care safely and respectfully.','Provide oral care for conscious and unconscious residents and denture care.','Assist with grooming, dressing, toileting and positioning.','Observe, report and document personal care accurately.'],sections:[
['Bathing and skin care',[
'Bathing cleans the skin, stimulates circulation, offers exercise and gives a chance to observe the skin. Types include the **complete bed bath**, **partial bath** (face, hands, underarms, perineum), **tub bath**, **shower** and **towel or bag baths**. Principles: provide **privacy** and warmth, check water temperature (generally **no hotter than about 105–110°F**, or as facility policy directs; have the resident test it), wash from **cleanest to dirtiest** areas—eyes first (without soap, inner to outer corner, using a different part of the washcloth for each eye), then face, arms, chest, abdomen, legs, back and finally the **perineal area**—rinse and pat dry, especially between toes and skin folds, and apply lotion as allowed (not between toes). **Perineal care** is done front to back for females and from the urethral opening outward for males, using a clean area of the washcloth for each stroke. Observe and report redness, rashes, bruises, swelling, breaks in the skin and areas of pressure.',
order('Order of a complete bed bath',['Eyes (no soap, inner to outer corner)','Face, ears and neck','Arms, hands and underarms','Chest and abdomen','Legs and feet','Back and buttocks','Perineal area'])]],
['Oral care and denture care',[
'Oral care prevents infection, tooth decay and pneumonia, improves comfort and appetite and lets you observe the mouth. For **conscious** residents, brush with a soft brush and fluoride toothpaste, floss as able and offer mouthwash. For **unconscious** residents, position them **on their side** with the head turned to prevent **aspiration**, use **minimal liquid** on swabs or a soft brush, never put fingers in the mouth and provide care often, as the care plan directs. **Dentures** are handled over a sink **lined with a towel or washcloth and filled with water** to prevent breakage, cleaned with a denture brush and cool water (hot water can warp them), stored in a labeled denture cup with water or solution and returned or stored as the resident prefers. Report sores, bleeding gums, loose teeth, ill-fitting dentures and mouth odor.',
sort('Correct oral care practice?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Turning an unconscious resident’s head to the side',0],['Cleaning dentures with hot water',1],['Lining the sink with a washcloth before cleaning dentures',0],['Putting fingers in an unconscious resident’s mouth',1],['Using a soft-bristled brush',0],['Storing dentures dry in a tissue',1]])]],
['Grooming, dressing and toileting',[
'Grooming supports dignity: comb and style hair as the resident prefers, shave with an **electric razor** for residents on anticoagulants (blood thinners) or as policy directs, clean and file fingernails, and leave toenail cutting to the nurse or podiatrist, especially for residents with diabetes or poor circulation. When **dressing**, offer choices, encourage independence, dress the **weak or affected side first** and undress the **strong side first**, and use adaptive devices. Assist with **toileting** on a regular schedule, provide privacy, offer the bedpan (a **fracture pan** for residents who cannot raise their hips) or urinal, give perineal care and hand hygiene afterward, and record output when ordered. Respond quickly to call lights for toileting—delays lead to incontinence and falls. Note and report the color, amount and consistency of urine and stool and any changes.',
match('Match the situation to the correct approach',[['Resident on anticoagulants needs a shave','Use an electric razor'],['Resident with diabetes has long toenails','Report to the nurse; do not cut them'],['Resident with a weak left arm','Dress the left arm first'],['Resident who cannot lift the hips','Use a fracture pan'],['Resident chooses a different shirt','Respect the choice'],['After toileting','Provide perineal care and hand hygiene']])]],
['Positioning and comfort',[
'Residents who cannot move themselves are repositioned **at least every two hours** in bed (or as the care plan directs). Positions include **supine** (on the back), **lateral** (side-lying, with pillows supporting the back, between the knees and under the upper arm), **prone** (on the abdomen), **Sims’** (left side with the right knee bent toward the chest), **Fowler’s** (head of bed raised 45–60°; semi-Fowler’s 30–45°) and **orthopneic** (sitting forward over the overbed table, for breathing difficulty). Keep the body in **good alignment**, support joints, prevent **foot drop** with footboards or supports and protect bony areas. Move residents using a **lift or draw sheet** with help—never drag—to prevent **shearing** and friction. Comfort measures include backrubs (unless contraindicated), adjusting room temperature and lighting, offering fluids and reporting pain promptly.',
match('Match the position to its description',[['Supine','Lying flat on the back'],['Fowler’s','Head of bed raised about 45–60°'],['Lateral','Lying on the side with pillow supports'],['Prone','Lying on the abdomen'],['Sims’','Left side with the right knee bent toward the chest'],['Orthopneic','Sitting and leaning forward to breathe more easily']])]],
['Reporting care',[
'Every task is also an observation. During care, notice **skin** condition, **appetite** and fluid intake, **elimination**, **mobility**, **mood and behavior**, **pain** and **changes from baseline**. Report changes to the nurse promptly—immediately for emergencies—and document what you did and observed: the time, the care given, objective findings and the resident’s statements in their own words. Never document care before it is given, and never document care you did not provide. Accurate records protect residents and staff and help the nurse update the care plan.',
kase('Observations during a bath',{a:{text:'During a bed bath, you notice a new bruise on a resident’s upper arm and she winces when you touch it. What should you do?',choices:[['Finish gently, report the bruise and her pain to the nurse and document objectively','b','Correct. Unexplained bruises and pain must be reported.',true],['Ignore it because older adults bruise easily','b','New bruises may indicate injury or abuse and must be reported.',false]]},b:{text:'How should you document it?',choices:[['“Bruise, purple, about 2 inches, left upper arm. Resident states, ‘It hurts when you touch it.’ Reported to nurse at 0915.”','c','Correct. Objective, specific and timed.',true],['“Resident was probably abused.”','c','Document facts, not conclusions.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse assesses the arm and follows facility policy for investigating the injury.',end:true}})]],
["Sleep, pain and comfort",["Rest and sleep restore the body and mind, but residents in facilities often sleep poorly because of noise, light, pain, anxiety, medications and changes in routine. Promote sleep by following the resident’s usual bedtime routine, reducing noise and light at night, offering toileting before bed, providing a backrub if allowed, ensuring a comfortable temperature and bedding, limiting caffeine late in the day and grouping care to avoid unnecessary interruptions. Report sleep problems, because they can signal pain, depression or other illness.","**Pain** is whatever the person says it is. Ask about location, intensity (0–10), quality and what makes it better or worse, and watch for nonverbal signs. Report pain promptly so the nurse can assess and treat it, and use **comfort measures** the care plan allows: repositioning, support with pillows, warm or cool applications only as ordered, relaxation, distraction, music and a calm environment. After the nurse treats pain, notice whether the resident is more comfortable and report the result.",{"t":"check","q":"A resident with dementia who cannot describe pain is grimacing and guarding her right hip. What should you do?","options":["Report the nonverbal signs of pain to the nurse","Assume she is fine because she did not complain","Give her an over-the-counter pain reliever","Encourage her to walk it off"],"why":"Nonverbal signs are important pain indicators, especially in dementia."}]]
],takeaways:['Bathe from cleanest to dirtiest, check water temperature, pat dry skin folds and observe skin; perineal care front to back.','Oral care for unconscious residents: side-lying, minimal liquid, no fingers in the mouth; clean dentures over a lined, water-filled sink with cool water.','Dress the weak side first, use electric razors for residents on blood thinners, reposition every 2 hours, never drag, and report and document observations objectively.'],terms:[['Perineal care','Cleaning of the genital and anal area.'],['Aspiration','Inhaling food, fluid or secretions into the lungs.'],['Foot drop','Weakness causing the foot to point downward.']],mcqs:[q('During a complete bed bath, which area is washed first?','Eyes','Feet','Perineal area','Back','Wash from cleanest to dirtiest.'),q('Eyes are washed:','Without soap, from the inner to the outer corner','With soap, outer to inner','Last','With the same part of the cloth for both','Use a clean area for each eye.'),q('For oral care on an unconscious resident, position them:','On their side with the head turned','Flat on the back','Sitting upright without support','Prone with the face down','Prevents aspiration.'),q('Dentures should be cleaned with:','Cool water over a lined, water-filled sink','Hot water','Bleach','Dry brushing only','Hot water can warp them.'),q('A resident taking blood thinners should be shaved with:','An electric razor','A straight razor','A safety razor only','No shaving allowed ever','Reduces bleeding risk.'),q('A resident who cannot raise their hips should use a:','Fracture pan','Standard bedpan','Urinal only','Commode','It slides under easily.'),q('Residents who cannot move themselves should be repositioned at least every:','2 hours','8 hours','12 hours','24 hours','Or as the care plan directs.'),q('Perineal care for female residents is done:','Front to back','Back to front','In circles','Only with dry cloths','Prevents urinary tract infection.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would provide a complete bed bath and oral care for a resident who is mostly bedbound, and explain what you would observe, report and document.',rubric:[['Bathing technique',['privacy','water temperature','cleanest','eyes','inner','perineal','pat dry']],['Oral care',['soft brush','denture','cool water','lined','side','aspiration']],['Skin and observations',['redness','pressure','bruise','skin','swelling','pain']],['Reporting and documentation',['report','nurse','objective','own words','time','document']]],model:'I would perform hand hygiene, explain, close the curtain and keep the resident warm and covered, checking the water temperature and having the resident test it. I would wash from cleanest to dirtiest: the eyes first without soap from the inner to outer corner with a clean part of the washcloth for each, then the face, ears and neck, arms and underarms, chest and abdomen, legs and feet, back and buttocks and finally the perineal area front to back, rinsing and patting dry, especially between the toes and in skin folds, and applying lotion as allowed. For oral care, I would brush with a soft brush and fluoride toothpaste and offer mouthwash, or for dentures, clean them over a sink lined with a washcloth and water using cool water and store them in a labeled cup; if the resident were unconscious, I would turn the head to the side and use minimal liquid. Throughout, I would observe the skin for redness that does not fade, pressure areas, bruises, rashes and swelling, and the mouth for sores, bleeding or poorly fitting dentures, along with pain and mood. I would report changes to the nurse promptly and document the care, the time, objective findings and the resident’s own words.'},sources:[RULES,NIDCR_OLD,CDC_CAUTI]};

export const A10={title:'Disabilities, disorders and end-of-life care',hook:'Disabilities and disorders are 20% of the test. Nursing assistants care for residents with dementia, mental health conditions, developmental disabilities and chronic illnesses, and they are often present at the end of life—roles that call for patience, skill and compassion.',objectives:['Care for residents with dementia and Alzheimer’s disease using person-centered techniques.','Support residents with mental health conditions and developmental disabilities.','Promote rehabilitation and independence for residents with chronic conditions.','Provide care for the dying resident and postmortem care with dignity.'],sections:[
['Dementia and Alzheimer’s disease',[
'**Dementia** is a serious, progressive loss of mental abilities—memory, thinking, reasoning and communication—that interferes with daily life; **Alzheimer’s disease** is the most common cause. **Delirium**, by contrast, is a **sudden, often reversible** confusion caused by infection, dehydration, medications or other illness, and must be reported right away. Residents with dementia may experience memory loss, confusion about time and place, **wandering**, **sundowning** (increased agitation in the late afternoon and evening), repetitive behaviors, **catastrophic reactions** (overreactions to stimuli) and difficulty with ADLs. Person-centered techniques: approach from the **front**, use the resident’s name, speak calmly in short sentences, give **one-step directions**, offer **simple choices**, keep **routines consistent**, break tasks into steps, use **validation** (respond to feelings rather than facts) and **redirection** instead of arguing, reduce noise and clutter and ensure safety with door alarms and supervision.',
sort('Dementia or delirium?',['Dementia','Delirium'],[['Gradual decline over months to years',0],['Sudden confusion over a day',1],['Often reversible when the cause is treated',1],['Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause',0],['May be caused by a urinary tract infection',1],['Progressive memory loss',0]])]],
['Mental health and developmental disabilities',[
'Mental health conditions are illnesses, not weaknesses. **Depression** (persistent sadness, loss of interest, sleep and appetite changes, hopelessness) is common but not normal in older adults; **anxiety** causes worry and physical symptoms; **bipolar disorder** includes depression and mania; **schizophrenia** may involve hallucinations or delusions; and **substance use disorders** affect people of every age. Nursing assistants listen without judgment, encourage participation in treatment and activities, do not argue with delusions, promote safety and **report any mention of suicide immediately**, staying with the resident if there is immediate danger. **Developmental disabilities**—such as intellectual disability, **Down syndrome**, **cerebral palsy** and **autism spectrum disorder**—begin before adulthood. Care focuses on the person’s abilities and preferences: use person-first language, communicate at the resident’s level, keep routines, use visual supports, break skills into steps and encourage independence.',
match('Match the condition to an appropriate approach',[['Depression','Encourage activities and report hopelessness'],['Anxiety','Stay calm and explain each step'],['Hallucinations','Do not argue; reassure and report'],['Autism spectrum disorder','Keep routines and reduce sensory overload'],['Cerebral palsy','Support positioning and encourage independence'],['Statement about suicide','Stay with the resident and report immediately']])]],
['Rehabilitation and chronic conditions',[
'**Rehabilitation** helps residents regain as much function and independence as possible after illness or injury such as stroke, hip fracture or amputation. The team—physical, occupational and speech therapists, nurses and nursing assistants—sets goals, and nursing assistants **reinforce** therapy by encouraging residents to do what they can, using **adaptive devices** (reachers, sock aids, built-up utensils, walkers and canes), praising progress and being patient. **Restorative care** maintains abilities through ROM, walking programs and self-care. Chronic conditions such as **diabetes**, **COPD**, **heart failure**, **arthritis**, **kidney disease** and **HIV** require consistent routines, monitoring and reporting of changes. Clients with **HIV** are cared for with **standard precautions**; HIV is not spread by casual contact.',
chk('A resident recovering from a stroke is slowly buttoning his own shirt. What should the nursing assistant do?',['Encourage him and allow extra time, helping only as needed','Button it for him to save time','Tell him to hurry','Skip dressing today'],'Promoting independence supports rehabilitation.')]],
['The dying resident',[
'Care at the end of life focuses on **comfort, dignity and family support**. **Hospice** provides comfort care for people with a terminal illness. Grief may follow the **stages** described by Kübler-Ross—**denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance**—in any order. Comfort measures include gentle repositioning, frequent **mouth care** and moistening the lips, skin care, keeping the resident clean and dry, managing the environment with soft lighting and quiet, offering fluids if the resident can swallow, providing privacy for family and respecting **spiritual and cultural practices**. **Hearing** is believed to be one of the last senses lost, so continue to speak gently. Signs of approaching death include decreased intake, cool and **mottled** skin, changes in breathing such as **Cheyne-Stokes respirations**, noisy breathing, decreased urine output and decreased responsiveness. **Advance directives** and **DNR (do-not-resuscitate)** orders must be followed.',
flip('End-of-life care',[['Mouth care','Frequent; moisten the lips'],['Hearing','May remain; speak gently'],['Skin','Cool and mottled hands and feet near death'],['Breathing','Cheyne-Stokes or noisy breathing'],['DNR order','Do not start CPR; follow the order'],['Family','Offer privacy and support']])]],
['Postmortem care',[
'After death is pronounced and the nurse directs, nursing assistants may provide **postmortem care** following facility policy and cultural or religious customs. Steps commonly include: hand hygiene and gloves; providing privacy; placing the body **supine** with arms at the sides and a **pillow under the head** to prevent discoloration of the face; **closing the eyes** gently; inserting **dentures** if the policy allows, or labeling and sending them with the body; removing tubes only if instructed; bathing soiled areas and placing a clean pad; removing jewelry and **documenting belongings**, unless the family wishes items to remain; applying **identification tags** as the facility requires; covering the body with a clean sheet; and allowing the family time. Treat the body with **respect** and follow the family’s wishes. Nursing assistants may grieve too and should seek support.',
kase('A resident’s final hours',{a:{text:'A resident on hospice with a DNR order stops breathing while you are in the room. What should you do?',choices:[['Note the time, stay calm and notify the nurse immediately; do not start CPR','b','Correct. Follow the DNR order and notify the nurse.',true],['Begin CPR right away','b','A valid DNR order must be respected.',false]]},b:{text:'The family arrives and asks to spend time with the resident before postmortem care. What do you do?',choices:[['Provide privacy and time, respecting their customs, and offer support','c','Correct. Family needs and customs come first.',true],['Ask them to wait outside while you finish quickly','c','Families should be given time with their loved one.',false]]},c:{text:'After the family’s time, you provide postmortem care with dignity according to policy and document the time and belongings.',end:true}})]],
["Responding to difficult behaviors",["Behaviors such as **agitation**, **aggression**, **wandering**, **pacing**, **repetitive questions**, **hoarding**, **inappropriate sexual behavior** and **refusing care** often communicate an unmet need—pain, hunger, thirst, a full bladder, fear, boredom, overstimulation or loneliness. Look for the **trigger** (what happened before), the **behavior** and the **result**, and report patterns to the nurse so the care plan can address them. Responses include staying calm, speaking softly, approaching from the front, keeping a safe distance from an aggressive resident, removing triggers such as noise, offering choices and comfort, redirecting to a meaningful activity and leaving and returning later if safe. Never argue, scold, restrain or threaten a resident, and get help if anyone is at risk.","Some behaviors can be prevented with consistent caregivers and routines, regular toileting, adequate fluids and snacks, meaningful activities, good lighting and quiet evenings. Documenting what works—such as a particular song that calms a resident—helps the whole team provide person-centered care.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Helpful or harmful response?","buckets":["Helpful","Harmful"],"items":[["Checking whether an agitated resident needs the bathroom",0],["Arguing with a resident who insists it is 1975",1],["Redirecting a wandering resident to a walk with you",0],["Scolding a resident for repeating questions",1],["Reducing noise in a crowded day room",0],["Threatening to withhold dessert",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Dementia is gradual and progressive; delirium is sudden and often reversible—report it; use one-step directions, choices, routine, validation and redirection.','Report any mention of suicide immediately; use person-first language and promote independence for residents with developmental disabilities; reinforce rehabilitation.','End of life: comfort, mouth care, speak gently, recognize signs of approaching death, follow DNR orders; postmortem care with dignity and respect for customs.'],terms:[['Delirium','Sudden, often reversible confusion caused by illness or medication.'],['Rehabilitation','Restoring function and independence after illness or injury.'],['Postmortem care','Care of the body after death.']],mcqs:[q('Which condition has a sudden onset and is often reversible?','Delirium','Alzheimer’s disease','Vascular dementia','Down syndrome','Report sudden confusion.'),q('Increased agitation in the late afternoon is called:','Sundowning','Delirium tremens','Catastrophic reaction','Aphasia','A calm routine helps.'),q('Which technique helps a resident with dementia?','Give one-step directions','Argue to correct confusion','Change routines daily','Give many choices at once','Simplify communication.'),q('A resident says, “I want to end my life.” You should:','Stay with the resident and report immediately','Ignore it','Promise to keep it secret','Change the subject','Suicide statements are always taken seriously.'),q('Which sense may remain until near death?','Hearing','Sight','Taste','Smell','Continue speaking gently.'),q('If a resident with a DNR order stops breathing, the nursing assistant should:','Notify the nurse and not start CPR','Start CPR','Call the family first','Leave the room','Follow the order.'),q('During postmortem care, a pillow is placed under the head to:','Prevent discoloration of the face','Keep the body warm','Close the mouth','It is not used','Blood pools in dependent areas.'),q('Rehabilitation encourages residents to:','Do as much as they can for themselves','Rely on staff for everything','Stay in bed','Avoid adaptive devices','Independence is the goal.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would care for a resident with moderate Alzheimer’s disease who becomes agitated in the evenings, and how your approach would change as the resident approaches the end of life.',rubric:[['Dementia understanding',['dementia','alzheimer','progressive','sundown','delirium']],['Person-centered techniques',['front','name','one-step','choice','routine','redirect','validat']],['End-of-life comfort care',['comfort','mouth care','reposition','hearing','privacy','family','spiritual']],['Signs and orders',['mottled','cheyne','intake','dnr','advance directive','report']]],model:'Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause of dementia, a gradual, progressive loss of memory and reasoning; I would also watch for sudden changes that might be delirium from an infection and report them. To manage evening agitation (sundowning), I would keep a calm, consistent late-afternoon routine with good lighting and reduced noise, approach from the front using her name, speak slowly in short sentences, give one-step directions and simple choices, validate her feelings and redirect her to a soothing, familiar activity rather than arguing, and keep her safe from wandering. As she approaches the end of life, my focus would shift to comfort and dignity: gentle repositioning, frequent mouth care and moist lips, keeping her clean and dry, soft lighting and quiet, offering sips only if she can swallow, continuing to speak gently because hearing may remain, giving the family privacy and respecting spiritual and cultural practices. I would watch for and report signs that death is near—decreased intake, cool mottled skin, Cheyne-Stokes or noisy breathing and decreased responsiveness—and follow her advance directive and DNR order.'},sources:[RULES,NIA_ALZ,NIMH,NIA_HOSPICE]};
